<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">
  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net11.0</TargetFramework>
    <AllowUnsafeBlocks>true</AllowUnsafeBlocks>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <IsAotCompatible>true</IsAotCompatible>
  </PropertyGroup>
  <ItemGroup>
    <Content Include="..\..\..\..\..\target\debug\*reference_project*">
      <CopyToOutputDirectory>PreserveNewest</CopyToOutputDirectory>
    </Content>
  </ItemGroup>
  <!--
      The Content glob above copies the native library these bindings P/Invoke into. It is
      correct as written: a top-level `cargo build -p reference_project` puts the cdylib in
      target\debug. Verified 2026-08-27 - do not "fix" the path. Building the crate only as a
      test dependency leaves an unhashed copy in target\debug\deps with no hardlink up, which
      is why the directory can look empty.

      Provisioning is deliberately NOT done from MSBuild. An Exec running cargo here contends
      with any other cargo holding the target directory lock; observed 2026-08-27 as
      `failed to create file target\debug\reference_project.d`, cargo exit 101, in a plain
      `dotnet build` with no outer cargo at all. Under `cargo test` it would block rather than
      fail. Provision on the cargo side: the Rust test reference_project::csharp_suite copies the
      cdylib from target\debug\deps up to target\debug before invoking dotnet, and asserts
      afterwards that it reached this project's output directory. Running that test is the
      supported way to build this project.

      A fail-fast guard was attempted 2026-08-27 and removed. Hooked to
      BeforeTargets="PrepareForBuild", it did not fire on an up-to-date project - that target is
      skipped, and the guard with it. If you retry, hook something that always runs and verify it
      fires in BOTH directions before trusting it. Until then the failure mode is
      DllNotFoundException from the Interop static constructor, which looks like a marshalling
      fault and is not.

      An earlier version of this comment said the all-features flag was mandatory here, because a
      narrower build supposedly yields EntryPointNotFoundException. That is wrong and was never
      observed. reference_project declares no features of its own and already requests the
      complete interoptopus feature set, so the all-features flag cannot change one byte of this cdylib;
      verified 2026-08-28, including that no cfg(feature) appears anywhere in its sources. If a
      missing export is ever suspected again, dump the export table and compare - do not infer it
      from build flags. See docs/csharp-unions-handoff.md section 9.
    -->
</Project>